## sn45.50-54
sn45.50-54:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, the dawn is the forerunner and harbinger of the sunrise. | T: “Bhikkhus, this is the forerunner and precursor of the rising of the sun, that is, the dawn.
sn45.50-54:1.2 | S: In the same way accomplishment in ethics is the forerunner and harbinger of the arising of the noble eightfold path for a mendicant. | T: So too, bhikkhus, for a bhikkhu this is the forerunner and precursor for the arising of the Noble Eightfold Path, that is, accomplishment in virtue … accomplishment in desire … accomplishment in self … accomplishment in view … accomplishment in diligence … … accomplishment in careful attention.
sn45.50-54:1.3 | S: A mendicant accomplished in ethics can expect to develop and cultivate the noble eightfold path. …” … | T: When a bhikkhu is accomplished in careful attention, it is to be expected that he will develop and cultivate this Noble Eightfold Path. “And how does a bhikkhu who is accomplished in careful attention develop and cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path? Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops right view, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release…. He develops right concentration, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release. It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu who is accomplished in careful attention develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path.”
sn45.50-54:1.4 | S: “… accomplishment in enthusiasm …” | T: (none)
sn45.50-54:1.5 | S: “… accomplishment in self-development …” | T: (none)
sn45.50-54:1.6 | S: “… accomplishment in view …” | T: (none)
sn45.50-54:1.7 | S: “… accomplishment in diligence …” | T: (none)
sn45.50-54:1.8 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn46.15
sn46.15:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Rajagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the Squirrel Sanctuary.
sn46.15:1.2 | S: Now at that time Venerable Mahāmoggallāna was staying on the Vulture’s Peak Mountain, and he was sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: Now on that occasion the Venerable Mahamoggallana was dwelling on Mount Vulture Peak—sick, afflicted, gravely ill.
sn46.15:1.3 | S: Then in the late afternoon, the Buddha came out of retreat, went to Venerable Moggallāna, sat down on the seat spread out, and said to him: | T: Then, in the evening, the Blessed One emerged from seclusion and approached the Venerable Mahamoggallana …
sn46.15:2.1 | S: “I hope you’re keeping well, Moggallāna, I hope you’re all right. I hope your pain is fading, not growing; that its fading is evident, not its growing.” | T: (none)
sn46.15:2.2 | S: “Sir, I’m not keeping well, I’m not getting by. My pain is terrible and growing, not fading; its growing is evident, not its fading.” | T: (none)
sn46.15:3.1 | S: “Moggallāna, I’ve rightly explained these seven awakening factors. When developed and cultivated, they lead to direct knowledge, to awakening, and to extinguishment. | T: (none)
sn46.15:3.2 | S: What seven? | T: all as above,
sn46.15:3.3 | S: The awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, and equanimity. | T: with the change of names being the only difference …
sn46.15:3.4 | S: These are the seven awakening factors that I’ve rightly explained. When developed and cultivated, they lead to direct knowledge, to awakening, and to extinguishment.” | T: (none)
sn46.15:3.5 | S: “Indeed, Blessed One, these are awakening factors! | T: (none)
sn46.15:3.6 | S: Indeed, Holy One, these are awakening factors!” | T: (none)
sn46.15:4.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: (none)
sn46.15:4.2 | S: Satisfied, Venerable Mahāmoggallāna approved what the Buddha said. | T: (none)
sn46.15:4.3 | S: And that’s how he recovered from that illness. | T: In such a way the Venerable Mahamoggallana was cured of his illness.
sn46.15:4.4 | P: Tathāpahīno cāyasmato mahāmoggallānassa so ābādho ahosīti. | T: (none)
sn46.15:4.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn11.12
sn11.12:1.1 | S: Near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove. | T: At Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove.
sn11.12:1.2 | S: There the Buddha said to the mendicants: | T: There the Blessed One said to the bhikkhus:
sn11.12:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, in a former life, when Sakka was a human being, he was a student named Magha. That’s why he’s called Maghavā, the Bounteous. | T: “Bhikkhus, in the past, when Sakka, lord of the devas, was a human being, he was a brahmin youth named Magha; therefore he is called Maghava.
sn11.12:2.1 | S: In a former life, when Sakka was a human being, he was first to give gifts. That’s why he’s called Purindada, the Firstgiver. | T: “Bhikkhus, in the past, when Sakka, lord of the devas, was a human being, he gave gifts in city after city; therefore he is called Purindada, the Urban Giver.
sn11.12:3.1 | S: In a former life, when Sakka was a human being, he gave gifts ably. That’s why he’s called Sakka, the Able. | T: “Bhikkhus, in the past, when Sakka, lord of the devas, was a human being, he gave gifts considerately; therefore he is called Sakka.
sn11.12:4.1 | S: In a former life, when Sakka was a human being, he gave the gift of a guest house. That’s why he’s called Vāsava, the Houser. | T: “Bhikkhus, in the past, when Sakka, lord of the devas, was a human being, he gave a rest house; therefore he is called Vasava.
sn11.12:5.1 | S: Sakka thinks of a thousand needs in an hour. That’s why he’s called Sahassakkha, the Thousand-Eyed. | T: “Bhikkhus, Sakka, lord of the devas, thinks of a thousand matters in a moment; therefore he is called Sahassakkha, Thousand-eyed.
sn11.12:6.1 | S: Sakka’s wife is the titan maiden named Sujā. That’s why he’s called Sujampati, Sujā’s Husband. | T: “Bhikkhus, Sakka’s wife is the asura maiden named Suja; therefore he is called Sujampati, Suja’s husband.
sn11.12:7.1 | S: Sakka rules as sovereign lord over the gods of the thirty-three. That’s why he’s called Devānaminda, the Lord of Gods. | T: “Bhikkhus, Sakka, lord of the devas, exercises supreme sovereignty and rulership over the Tavatiṁsa devas; therefore he is called lord of the devas.
sn11.12:8.1 | S: In a former life, when Sakka was a human being, he undertook seven vows. And it was because of undertaking these that he achieved the status of Sakka. | T: (none)
sn11.12:8.2 | S: What seven? | T: (none)
sn11.12:8.3 | S: As long as I live, may I support my parents. | T: (none)
sn11.12:8.4 | S: As long as I live, may I honor the elders in the family. | T: (none)
sn11.12:8.5 | S: As long as I live, may I speak gently. | T: (none)
sn11.12:8.6 | S: As long as I live, may I not backbite. | T: (none)
sn11.12:8.7 | S: As long as I live, may I live at home with heart rid of the stain of stinginess, freely generous, open-handed, loving to let go, committed to charity, loving to give and to share. | T: (none)
sn11.12:8.8 | S: As long as I live, may I speak the truth. | T: (none)
sn11.12:8.9 | S: As long as I live, may I be free of anger, or should anger arise, may I quickly get rid of it. | T: (none)
sn11.12:8.10 | S: In a former life, when Sakka was a human being, he undertook seven vows. And it was because of undertaking these that he achieved the status of Sakka. | T: “Bhikkhus, in the past, when Sakka, lord of the devas, was a human being, he adopted and undertook seven vows by the undertaking of which he achieved the status of Sakka….”
sn11.12:9.1 | S: A child who respects their parents, | T: The remainder of this sutta is identical with the preceding one.
sn11.12:9.2 | S: and honors the elders in the family, | T: (none)
sn11.12:9.3 | S: whose speech is gentle and courteous, | T: (none)
sn11.12:9.4 | S: and has given up backbiting; | T: (none)
sn11.12:10.1 | S: who’s committed to getting rid of stinginess, | T: (none)
sn11.12:10.2 | S: is truthful, and has mastered anger: | T: (none)
sn11.12:10.3 | S: the gods of the thirty-three declare | T: (none)
sn11.12:10.4 | S: them to be a true person.” | T: Verses 906–7 = 904–5.

## sn35.95
sn35.95:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Māluṅkyaputta went up to the Buddha … and asked him, | T: Then the Venerable Maluṅkyaputta approached the Blessed One … and said to him:
sn35.95:1.2 | S: “Sir, may the Buddha please teach me Dhamma in brief. When I’ve heard it, I’ll live alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute.” | T: “Venerable sir, it would be good if the Blessed One would teach me the Dhamma in brief, so that, having heard the Dhamma from the Blessed One, I might dwell alone, withdrawn, diligent, ardent, and resolute.”
sn35.95:2.1 | S: “Well now, Māluṅkyaputta, what are we to say to the young monks, | T: “Here now, Maluṅkyaputta, what should I say to the young bhikkhus when a bhikkhu like you—
sn35.95:2.2 | S: when even an old man like you, elderly and senior, advanced in years, having reached the final stage of life, asks for brief advice?” | T: old, aged, burdened with years, advanced in life, come to the last stage—asks me for an exhortation in brief?”
sn35.95:3.1 | S: “Sir, even though I’m an old man, elderly and senior, | T: “Although, venerable sir, I am old, aged, burdened with years, advanced in life, come to the last stage,
sn35.95:3.2 | S: may the Buddha please teach me Dhamma in brief! May the Holy One please teach me in brief! Hopefully I can understand the meaning of what the Buddha says. Hopefully I can be an heir of the Buddha’s teaching!” | T: let the Blessed One teach me the Dhamma in brief, let the Fortunate One teach me the Dhamma in brief. Perhaps I may understand the meaning of the Blessed One’s statement, perhaps I may become an heir to the Blessed One’s statement.”
sn35.95:4.1 | S: “What do you think, Māluṅkyaputta? | T: “What do you think, Maluṅkyaputta,
sn35.95:4.2 | S: Do you have any desire or greed or fondness for sights known by the eye that are unseen, unseen before, and which you neither see nor think might be seen?” | T: do you have any desire, lust, or affection for those forms cognizable by the eye that you have not seen and never saw before, that you do not see and would not think might be seen?”
sn35.95:4.3 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, venerable sir.”
sn35.95:5.1 | S: “Do you have any desire or greed or affection for sounds known by the ear … | T: “Do you have any desire, lust, or affection for those sounds cognizable by the ear …
sn35.95:5.2 | P: “No hetaṁ, bhante”. | T: (none)
sn35.95:6.1 | S: smells known by the nose … | T: for those odours cognizable by the nose …
sn35.95:6.2 | P: “No hetaṁ, bhante”. | T: (none)
sn35.95:7.1 | S: tastes known by the tongue … | T: for those tastes cognizable by the tongue …
sn35.95:7.2 | P: “No hetaṁ, bhante”. | T: (none)
sn35.95:8.1 | S: touches known by the body … | T: for those tactile objects cognizable by the body …
sn35.95:8.2 | P: “No hetaṁ, bhante”. | T: (none)
sn35.95:9.1 | S: ideas known by the mind that are unknown, unknown before, and which you neither know nor think might be known?” | T: for those mental phenomena cognizable by the mind that you have not cognized and never cognized before, that you do not cognize and would not think might be cognized?”
sn35.95:9.2 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, venerable sir.”
sn35.95:10.1 | S: “In that case, when it comes to things that ought be seen, heard, thought, and known: in the seen will be merely the seen; in the heard will be merely the heard; in the thought will be merely the thought; in the known will be merely the known. | T: “Here, Maluṅkyaputta, regarding things seen, heard, sensed, and cognized by you: in the seen there will be merely the seen; in the heard there will be merely the heard; in the sensed there will be merely the sensed; in the cognized there will be merely the cognized.
sn35.95:10.2 | S: When this is the case, | T: “When, Maluṅkyaputta, regarding things seen, heard, sensed, and cognized by you, in the seen there will be merely the seen, in the heard there will be merely the heard, in the sensed there will be merely the sensed, in the cognized there will be merely the cognized,
sn35.95:10.3 | S: you won’t be ‘by that’. | T: then, Maluṅkyaputta, you will not be ‘by that.’
sn35.95:10.4 | S: When you’re not ‘by that’, | T: When, Maluṅkyaputta, you are not ‘by that,’
sn35.95:10.5 | S: you won’t be ‘in that’. | T: then you will not be ‘therein. ’
sn35.95:10.6 | S: When you’re not ‘in that’, | T: When, Maluṅkyaputta, you are not ‘therein,’
sn35.95:10.7 | S: you won’t be in this life or the next or in between the two. | T: then you will be neither here nor beyond nor in between the two.
sn35.95:10.8 | S: Just this is the end of suffering.” | T: This itself is the end of suffering.”
